WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:05.900
Within an organism, each
non-reproductive cell contains all of

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:11.620
the DNA of that organism. This DNA
encodes all the proteins made in the

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:12.260
organism.

4
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.250
But if all cells have all of the DNA,
how do different cells do different

5
00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.250
things? How does a brain cell make only
the proteins needed for a brain to

6
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.630
function correctly, but not the
proteins that are only needed in a

7
00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.090
muscle? So how does a cell decide which
genes it should express and which it

8
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.260
should not? The answer: transcription
factors.

9
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.710
Transcription factors are proteins that
scan along the DNA, looking for

10
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:43.830
specific DNA sequences to bind. Once
they find and bind their specific DNA

11
00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.350
target, they change shape and can
interact with or recruit other

12
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:47.960
proteins.

13
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.980
Through this binding and interaction
with other proteins, these

14
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:59.090
transcription factors can increase,
turn on, or decrease, turn off, the

15
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.950
expression of specific genes.

16
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.610
The main way they do this is to promote
or block the recruitment of RNA

17
00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.260
polymerase, the enzyme that transcribes
DNA into RNA.

18
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.890
If the transcription factor promotes
the recruitment of RNA polymerase, it

19
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:18.690
is helping to turn that gene on. In
this case, RNA polymerase will bind

20
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:23.090
more easily and frequently to the gene
to which the transcription factor is

21
00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:23.810
associated.

22
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.790
This results in an increase in
the amount of RNA produced from that

23
00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:32.250
gene. However, if the transcription
factor blocks the recruitment of RNA

24
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.140
polymerase, it is helping to turn that
gene off. Therefore, RNA polymerase

25
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.550
will not bind as easily or frequently
to the associated gene and there'll be

26
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.960
little or no RNA produced from that
gene in this cell.

27
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:50.680
Since the amount of RNA produced by RNA
polymerase is usually related to the

28
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.600
amount of protein made, transcription
factors help control which genes are

29
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.820
expressed in which cells, and therefore
helps cells perform specialized

30
00:01:58.820 --> 00:01:59.460
functions.

